-- Plan 61 §11.2: a `where` entry that defines no name, refused.
--
-- §6 of the plan said "`where` is a declaration group and a block is a
-- statement sequence; this row is about the former only", and §11.2 found
-- that the parser said otherwise in its own words: `Clause.wheres` is a
-- `Vec<Stmt>` and `where_locals` pushed `self.stmt()`, so a group could hold
-- a print, an assignment binding no name, or anything else a statement can
-- be — all of which a sort can move.
--
-- The refusal is categorical rather than a restriction added to the
-- construct. A `where` group is a **set of definitions**, which is the whole
-- reason it can be sorted; an entry with an effect has no defined position in
-- a set.

g : u64 -> u64
g n = y
  where
    println! \-> "#{n}"
    y = n + 1
